{
  "dom_id": "step-q2547",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/d4c0faafff52f414c30a4da32f9f0ba3520ef88d7c8bebe5c12a7ccea3109d17.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5\\text{\u00a0cm}?$",
  "worked_solution": "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=5,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi $ and $r=5,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (5) \\\\ 40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 10\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $4$ centimeters per minute.",
